  "pop": 1412914090,
  "gov": "unitary one-party socialist republic under the Chinese Communist Party",
  "launchAuth": "The Party Central Military Commission, chaired by the CCP General Secretary (Xi Jinping since 2012), holds authority; the PLA Rocket Force (PLARF, formerly Second Artillery) executes. There is no publicly documented…",
  "tpnw": "not signed",
  "notes": [
   "FAS puts China at ~620 warheads at the start of 2026 — but only ~34 are 'deployed'. Almost the entire arsenal sits in central storage separate from its launchers, a fundamentally different posture from the US or Russia, which is why Chinese and American 'deployed' figures are not comparable.",
   "The three new silo fields total 320 silos: Yumen 120, Hami 110, Ordos/Hanggin Banner 90. All are structurally complete; the December 2025 DoD report says more than 100 have been loaded with DF-31-class ICBMs.",
   "China's ~350 new silos exceed the entire silo-based ICBM force Russia operates and amount to about three-quarters of the whole US ICBM force — the largest expansion of the Chinese arsenal ever undertaken.",
   "FAS states plainly that it cannot reconcile the 600-plus stockpile figure with China's observable force structure unless a large number of warheads are assigned to the new silos — i.e. the headline number is partly an inference about warheads produced for missiles not yet fielded.",
   "The 3 September 2025 Beijing parade was the first time China publicly displayed a complete nuclear triad, revealing the previously unknown DF-61 road-mobile ICBM and the silo-based DF-31BJ ('J' from Chinese jing, meaning well/silo).",
   "Separately from the new fields, China is expanding its liquid-fuel DF-5 silos from 18 toward roughly 48 across four brigades in Hunan and Henan, including two brand-new 12-silo fields likely intended for the multi-megaton DF-5C.",
   "Essentially the whole stockpile is centrally held at Base 67 (formerly Base 22) inside the Qinling mountains at Taibai, Shaanxi — two zones, Hongchuan and the Hongling command centre. A March 2026 CASI study characterised this concentration as a 'highly concentrated risk'.",
   "DF-26 launchers grew from 18 in 2018 to about 300 across eight brigades by 2026, yet FAS assigns only ~100 nuclear warheads to the force. The 646 Brigade at Korla is the first confirmed PLARF unit with simultaneous nuclear and conventional missions, with warheads reportedly swappable in the field.",
   "China stopped producing fissile material for weapons in the late 1980s, leaving roughly 14 tonnes of HEU and 2.9 tonnes of weapon-grade plutonium — enough for the current buildup but probably not beyond ~1,000 warheads without new production.",
   "The plutonium concern is now civil infrastructure: the two CFR-600 fast reactors at Xiapu (Fujian) and the Jinta reprocessing complex in Gansu, whose first 200 t/yr plant IPFM confirmed had started operating in May 2026. China has not reported its separated plutonium stockpile to the IAEA since 2017."
  ],
