  "narrative": "Pakistan began a nuclear weapons programme after the 1971 war and India's 1974 'Smiling Buddha' test, with Zulfikar Ali Bhutto's January 1972 Multan meeting as the political starting point; A. Q. Khan returned from URENCO in 1975 and built a gas-centrifuge enrichment complex at Kahuta, making Pakistan the only arsenal historically founded on highly enriched uranium rather than plutonium. Cold and subcritical tests were carried out in tunnels at Kirana Hills near Sargodha from 11 March 1983, and Pakistan tested openly on 28 and 30 May 1998 at Ras Koh in the Chagai district and in the Kharan desert, two weeks after India's Pokhran-II. As of January 2026 FAS, SIPRI and the Arms Control Association all put the stockpile at about 170 warheads - not deployed, but held de-mated in central storage - carried by roughly 162 launchers: 36 Mirage III/V fighter-bombers and about 126 road-mobile ballistic and ground-launched cruise missiles. The arsenal has been growing by perhaps 5-10 warheads a year and could reach about 200 by the late 2020s, fed by an unusually diverse fissile-material complex: gas centrifuges at Kahuta and Gadwal (about 5.5 tonnes of weapon-usable HEU), four unsafeguarded heavy-water plutonium production reactors at Khushab, and reprocessing at New Labs (Nilore) and Chashma yielding roughly 620 kg of weapon-grade plutonium. Weapons work is concentrated at the Pakistan…",
