  "narrative": "The United States built the first nuclear weapons under the Manhattan Project and detonated the Trinity device in the New Mexico desert on 16 July 1945, then used two bombs against Hiroshima and Nagasaki weeks later. It conducted 1,054 nuclear tests between 1945 and the 'Divider' shot of 23 September 1992, peaked at 31,255 warheads in 1967, and has cut its arsenal by roughly 85 percent since. As of 2026 FAS estimates a military stockpile of about 3,700 warheads - roughly 1,670 deployed strategic, about 100 tactical B61-12 bombs at six NATO bases in Europe, and around 1,930 in reserve - plus about 1,342 retired warheads queued for dismantlement at Pantex, for a total inventory near 5,042. Those weapons sit on 400 Minuteman III ICBMs in 450 silos across Wyoming/Nebraska/Colorado, Montana and North Dakota; on 14 Ohio-class submarines out of Bangor, Washington and Kings Bay, Georgia; and in storage vaults at Minot and Whiteman for the B-52H and B-2A bomber forces, with the enormous KUMMSC bunker complex at Kirtland AFB holding much of the reserve. The entire triad is being replaced at once - Sentinel for Minuteman III, Columbia-class for Ohio, B-21 Raider for the B-2, LRSO for the ALCM, plus the W87-1, W93, B61-12 and B61-13 warheads - at a cost the CBO puts near a trillion dollars over 30 years, while NNSA struggles to restart plutonium pit production at Los Alamos and Savannah…",
