  "narrative": "South Africa is the only state that built nuclear weapons entirely on its own and then destroyed them. Work began at the Somchem propellant plant near Cape Town in 1971-74 as a 'peaceful nuclear explosive' project, moved to Pelindaba in 1977, and was handed to the state arms company Armscor in 1979, which built the dedicated Kentron Circle weapons plant inside the Gerotek vehicle-test estate west of Pretoria. The fissile material came from the Y-Plant at Valindaba, a uniquely South African 'Helikon' aerodynamic vortex enrichment process that was expensive, unreliable and produced roughly 400 kg of HEU. The devices were simple gun-assembly weapons using about 55 kg of HEU each with a tungsten reflector, expected to yield 10-18 kt; six were completed and a seventh was partly built. Two shafts, 216 m and 385 m deep, were drilled at Vastrap in the Kalahari; Soviet and then American satellites spotted the site in August 1977 and international pressure forced South Africa to abandon a planned 'cold' instrumented test. The unexplained 22 September 1979 'Vela incident' double flash in the South Atlantic is still contested; most independent analysts believe it was a nuclear test, and many attribute it to Israel, but South Africa's own programme could not have tested a device at that date. F.W. de Klerk ordered termination in late 1989; dismantlement ran from July 1990, the HEU was…",
