  "narrative": "When the USSR dissolved in December 1991 Ukraine was left holding the world's third-largest nuclear arsenal on its territory: about 1,900 strategic warheads on 176 ICBMs (130 SS-19 Stiletto and 46 SS-24 Scalpel) plus 44 heavy bombers with roughly 1,080 air-launched cruise missiles, and a further 2,275-2,600 tactical warheads. Ukraine never had operational control - the launch codes and the 12th GUMO custodial units stayed Russian - but it did have physical possession, and in 1992-93 Kyiv argued that it was the 'administrative' owner and should be compensated. The tactical weapons went back to Russia by May 1992 in a chaotic, poorly coordinated transfer. Under the Lisbon Protocol of 23 May 1992 Ukraine, Belarus and Kazakhstan joined START I as non-nuclear-weapon states; the Trilateral Statement of January 1994 traded the warheads for compensation in nuclear fuel and debt relief; and on 5 December 1994 the Budapest Memorandum gave Ukraine assurances of its independence, sovereignty and existing borders from Russia, the United States and the United Kingdom in exchange for NPT accession. The last strategic warhead left Ukraine on 1 June 1996; the SS-24 silos at Pervomaysk were blown up with US Cooperative Threat Reduction money, one command post surviving as a museum. Russia's 2014 seizure of Crimea and 2022 full-scale invasion made the Budapest Memorandum a byword for worthless…",
