  "narrative": "Kazakhstan inherited about 1,410 Soviet strategic warheads - 104 SS-18 heavy ICBMs each carrying ten warheads at Derzhavinsk and Zhangiz-Tobe, plus roughly 370 air-launched cruise missiles for 40 Tu-95MS bombers at Chagan/Dolon - together with the Semipalatinsk Test Site, where 456 nuclear tests had been conducted between 1949 and 1989 and where fallout affected an estimated 1.5 million people. Popular revulsion at that legacy produced the Nevada-Semipalatinsk movement, and President Nursultan Nazarbayev closed the test site by decree on 29 August 1991, before the USSR itself had ended - a date the UN later adopted as the International Day against Nuclear Tests. Kazakhstan joined the Lisbon Protocol in May 1992, acceded to the NPT in February 1994, and the last strategic warheads left for Russia on 24 April 1995; the final Semipalatinsk test tunnel was sealed on 29 July 2000. Two other operations mattered: Project Sapphire in November 1994 airlifted 581 kg of poorly guarded weapons-grade HEU from the Ulba Metallurgical Plant at Oskemen to the Y-12 plant at Oak Ridge, and a long US-Russian-Kazakh secret operation completed in 2012 secured several hundred kilograms of plutonium-bearing debris in the Degelen tunnels. Kazakhstan hosts the IAEA's Low Enriched Uranium Bank at Ulba (opened 2017), chairs the Central Asian Nuclear-Weapon-Free Zone in 2026 - the zone's treaty was…",
