  "narrative": "Muammar Gaddafi tried for three decades to buy a bomb rather than build one - approaching China in 1970, seeking Soviet and Pakistani help, and reportedly funding others' programmes - before turning to the A.Q. Khan network. From 1997 Khan's network supplied Libya with P-1 and then P-2 centrifuge components, 1.7 tonnes of uranium hexafluoride, and in 2001-02 a set of Chinese-origin nuclear weapon design documents. Libya never got a working cascade: the equipment stayed largely in crates at warehouses around Tripoli, and the 10 MW Soviet IRT research reactor at Tajura remained the country's only real nuclear infrastructure. The end came quickly. Secret talks with British and American intelligence began in March 2003; in October 2003 the German-flagged BBC China was intercepted carrying five containers of Malaysian-made centrifuge parts; and on 19 December 2003 Libya publicly renounced nuclear, chemical and biological weapons and long-range missiles. On 22 January 2004 US transport aircraft flew about 25 tonnes of centrifuge components, UF6 and documents - including the weapon design - to Oak Ridge, Tennessee; the last equipment left in March 2004. In 2004-09 Russia removed the reactor's HEU fuel. The disarmament is often cited as a nonproliferation success and, since Gaddafi's overthrow and killing in 2011, just as often cited by North Korea and Iran as a reason never to…",
