  "narrative": "Iraq ran the most ambitious clandestine weapons programme ever mounted by an NPT member in good standing. It began with French and Italian help in the 1970s: the 40 MW Osiraq/Tammuz-1 research reactor at Tuwaitha, Italian hot cells, and a plan to separate plutonium. Israel destroyed Osiraq on 7 June 1981 in Operation Opera before it went critical - which, most historians now judge, pushed Iraq from a slow plutonium route towards a much larger covert enrichment effort. Under Petrochemical Project 3 (PC-3), Iraq pursued electromagnetic isotope separation (calutrons) at Tuwaitha, Al Tarmiya and Ash Sharqat, chemical enrichment at Tuwaitha, and gas centrifuges at Rashdiya and Al Furat, feeding a weaponisation programme at Al Atheer near Musayyib, where implosion lens development and a cold-test bunker were built. In 1990-91 Iraq launched a crash 'Petrochemical-4' effort to divert safeguarded French and Russian HEU research reactor fuel into a single device; it was nowhere near completion when the war began. The IAEA Action Team and UNSCOM, working under UN Security Council Resolution 687, uncovered the programme from mid-1991 onwards - the celebrated 'parking lot' standoff over calutron documents came in September 1991 - and demolished Al Atheer in April 1992. Iraq's remaining HEU was removed in 1994. No reconstituted programme existed by 2003; the invasion premised on WMD found…",
