  "narrative": "Taiwan pursued nuclear weapons twice, and both times the United States shut it down. After China's first test in 1964, Chiang Kai-shek authorised the 'Hsin Chu Programme'; the Institute of Nuclear Energy Research (INER) was founded at Longtan in 1968, and Canada supplied a 40 MW heavy-water Taiwan Research Reactor (TRR) modelled on the NRX, which went critical in 1973 and could produce significant plutonium. Taiwan also bought heavy water from the US, sought a reprocessing plant, and built hot cells. The IAEA and Washington caught the diversion of plutonium in 1976; Taipei pledged in September 1976 to abandon reprocessing and hand back the plutonium. Work quietly continued at INER and at the military Chungshan Institute of Science and Technology until Colonel Chang Hsien-yi, INER's deputy director and a long-term CIA source, defected to the United States in January 1988 with documentation showing Taiwan was within one to two years of a device. Washington forced the immediate shutdown and dismantlement of the TRR and the hot cells; the reactor was gutted under US supervision, and the roughly 863 kg of irradiated fuel and its 85 kg of plutonium were dealt with, with the last spent fuel shipped out for disposition in later decades. INER was renamed the National Atomic Research Institute in 2023. Taiwan remains a non-nuclear-weapon state under a safeguards agreement applied…",
