  "narrative": "President Park Chung-hee launched a covert weapons effort - often referred to as Project 890 - around 1972-73, driven by the Nixon Doctrine, the withdrawal of the US 7th Infantry Division and the fall of Saigon. The Agency for Defense Development and the Korea Atomic Energy Research Institute at Daejeon were the institutional homes; the technical route ran through a French pilot reprocessing plant and a Canadian NRX-type research reactor. Washington discovered the programme in late 1974 and applied intense pressure, including threats to the US-ROK nuclear cooperation agreement and to Export-Import Bank financing for Korean power reactors; Seoul cancelled the French contract in January 1976. Interest revived briefly after Park's assassination in 1979 and was suppressed again. Two undeclared experiments surfaced when South Korea implemented the IAEA Additional Protocol: a 1982 laboratory separation of about 270 micrograms of plutonium, and atomic vapour laser isotope separation experiments in 2000 that produced roughly 200 milligrams of uranium enriched on average to about 10 percent, with some material reaching 77 percent. Seoul disclosed these in August 2004; the IAEA found them a matter of serious concern but not part of an ongoing weapons programme. The debate is very much alive in 2026. The Asan Institute's February 2026 survey found 60.4 percent support for redeploying US…",
