  "status": "program",
  "narrative": "Sweden came closer to a bomb than any other European neutral. From 1945 the Defence Research Establishment (FOA) studied nuclear explosives under the cover of 'protection research', and from 1948 the state company AB Atomenergi built a domestic fuel cycle deliberately designed to be independent of foreign, safeguarded supply: natural-uranium heavy-water reactors fed by Swedish uranium from the Ranstad shale, plus a reprocessing capability. The R1 reactor under the Royal Institute of Technology in Stockholm went critical in 1954; the Agesta reactor near Stockholm supplied district heating from 1964; and the Marviken reactor was designed with an on-load refuelling capability well suited to plutonium production, though it never operated. FOA's Ursvik laboratory did plutonium metallurgy and criticality work; by the early 1960s Swedish planners believed a 100-warhead tactical arsenal for Saab A32 Lansen and later A37 Viggen aircraft was achievable within about a decade at a cost of a few percent of the defence budget. Politics killed it. The Social Democratic party split, a powerful women's movement campaigned against the bomb, and Sweden pivoted to championing disarmament - Alva Myrdal's diplomacy made non-proliferation a national brand. Parliament effectively barred weapons work in 1958-59 by restricting FOA to 'defensive' research, the option was formally abandoned in 1966-68,…",
