  "pursuedTo": 1988,
  "status": "program",
  "narrative": "Switzerland's bomb project was a secret that outlasted the Cold War in paper form. On 5 November 1945 Federal Councillor Karl Kobelt convened a conference that created the Study Commission for Atomic Energy (SKA) under ETH physicist Paul Scherrer; a confidential directive of 5 February 1946 instructed it to 'strive to develop a Swiss bomb'. In 1958 the Federal Council formally declared that the army should be equipped with nuclear weapons, and a 1963 military staff paper sketched an arsenal of some 200 bombs and shells. The technical base was the DIORIT heavy-water research reactor at Wurenlingen, which went critical in 1960 and could in principle produce plutonium, the ill-fated underground Lucens power reactor in Vaud, which suffered a partial meltdown and radioactive release in January 1969, and the AC-Laboratorium at Spiez. Switzerland tried to buy plutonium abroad and even sounded out the possibility of buying weapons. Two popular initiatives to ban nuclear weapons were rejected at referendum in 1962 and 1963, but cost, the lack of indigenous uranium, the Lucens accident and, decisively, signature of the NPT in 1969 hollowed the project out. Switzerland ratified the NPT in 1977. The working group was only formally dissolved in 1988, and the full story emerged from declassified files in the mid-1990s.",
