  "status": "program",
  "narrative": "Josip Broz Tito ordered nuclear weapons research within days of learning of the first Soviet test in 1949, and the Institute for Nuclear Sciences at Vinca, founded in 1948 on the Danube outside Belgrade, became the programme's home. Vinca built a heavy-water zero-power assembly (RB) in 1958 and, with Soviet help, the 6.5 MW heavy-water RA research reactor in 1959, together with a spent-fuel reprocessing department created in 1956 and hot cells. A criticality accident at the RB assembly in October 1958 killed one researcher and led to pioneering bone-marrow transplants in Paris. The weapons effort was shelved in the early 1960s as Yugoslavia embraced the Non-Aligned Movement and signed the NPT in 1968, but India's 1974 test - by Yugoslavia's rival for non-aligned leadership - revived it: 'Program A' under the Military Technical Institute pursued weapons-relevant work alongside 'Program B' for a civil fuel cycle, with Vinca acquiring 80 percent enriched Soviet fuel from 1976 and running the RA entirely on HEU from 1980. The presidency terminated Program A on 7 July 1987, and after Yugoslavia's break-up the successor states were left with the legacy: about 48 kg of fresh HEU was flown from Vinca to Russia in August 2002 under the Vinca Option, and 2.5 tonnes of spent HEU fuel, in 8,030 elements, were shipped to Mayak in a large IAEA-coordinated operation completed in December…",
