  "narrative": "Under military rule Brazil ran a 'Parallel Programme' (Programa Autonomo de Tecnologia Nuclear) outside IAEA safeguards, split between the Navy, the Air Force and the Army. The Navy's effort at the Aramar Experimental Centre near Ipero developed indigenous gas centrifuges - Brazil enriched uranium for the first time in 1982 - and a naval propulsion reactor; the Air Force pursued laser enrichment and, from 1979, prepared a test site; the Army worked on a graphite reactor at Solimoes. At the Campo de Provas Brigadeiro Velloso in the Serra do Cachimbo, Para, the Air Force drilled shafts, one about 320 m deep, widely reported as intended for an underground nuclear test; journalists exposed them in 1986. On 18 September 1990 President Fernando Collor de Mello flew to Cachimbo and shovelled lime into the shaft in a deliberate act of political theatre, symbolically burying the programme, and in 1990 a congressional inquiry exposed the parallel accounts. Brazil then built the regional architecture that replaced mistrust with verification: the 1991 Guadalajara agreement with Argentina creating ABACC, the joint Quadripartite Agreement with ABACC and the IAEA in force from 1994, full entry into force of the Treaty of Tlatelolco in 1994, and NPT accession in 1998. Brazil's constitution prohibits nuclear activity for non-peaceful purposes. It remains the only non-nuclear-weapon state…",
