  "narrative": "Algeria's case is the clearest example of a suspected programme that was defused by transparency rather than force. In February 1983 Algeria secretly contracted China to build a 15 MW heavy-water research reactor, Es Salam, at Birine near Ain Oussera, 250 km south of Algiers. Neither state told the IAEA. The Washington Times revealed the site on 11 April 1991 from satellite imagery, and what alarmed analysts was less the reactor than its context: heavy air defences around a remote desert site, a cooling capacity that appeared oversized for the declared power - suggesting the reactor could be run harder to make plutonium - and an associated hot-cell building capable of handling irradiated fuel. Algeria was not then an NPT member. The Bush administration pressed both Beijing and Algiers hard; China confirmed the deal in a note in late May 1991 and disclosed the supply of 11 tonnes of heavy water and 216 fuel assemblies. Algeria placed Es Salam under IAEA safeguards in 1992, acceded to the NPT in January 1995, and signed the Additional Protocol in 2018. Es Salam still operates, alongside the Argentine-supplied 1 MW NUR reactor at Draria near Algiers, which went critical in 1989. No evidence of an actual weapons design effort has been published; analysts' judgements ranged from a deliberate hedge to a prestige project procured with characteristic Algerian secrecy.",
