  "n": "Romania",
  "pursuedFrom": null,
  "pursuedTo": 1989,
  "status": "program",
  "narrative": "Romania ratified the NPT in 1970 and then quietly violated it. Nicolae Ceausescu, pursuing autonomy from Moscow, funded weapons-relevant research directed from the Institute of Atomic Physics at Magurele near Bucharest and carried out at the Institute for Nuclear Research at Pitesti-Mioveni, which operated a US-supplied TRIGA reactor and hot cells. In 1985 Romanian scientists secretly separated about 100 milligrams of plutonium from irradiated fuel and conducted undeclared uranium experiments - a laboratory demonstration rather than a weapons capability, but a clear safeguards breach. Romania also sought heavy water and enrichment technology and explored a CANDU-based route, and there were persistent reports of interest in acquiring know-how from abroad. The programme died with the regime: Ceausescu and his wife were executed on 25 December 1989, and the new government disclosed the plutonium experiment to the IAEA. Inspections in 1990 and April-May 1992 confirmed the undeclared work, and the material was placed under safeguards. Romania joined NATO in 2004 and the EU in 2007, hosts the US Aegis Ashore missile defence site at Deveselu, and its nuclear programme is now entirely civil: two CANDU units at Cernavoda, two more under construction, and a planned NuScale small modular reactor at Doicesti.",
