  "status": "exhost",
  "narrative": "Poland never owned nuclear weapons, but from 1970 to 1990 Soviet warheads were stored on Polish soil under an arrangement that mirrored NATO's nuclear sharing. Under the codename 'Wisla' (Vistula), agreed on 25 February 1967, Polish engineer units built three identical storage complexes to a Soviet design in remote forests: Object 3001 at Podborsko in West Pomerania, Object 3002 at Brzeznica-Kolonia in Greater Poland, and Object 3003 at Templewo in Lubusz. Each had two hardened Monolit-type bunkers with climate control, blast doors and multiple fence lines, and each could hold on the order of 100 to nearly 300 warheads - estimates for the total run to roughly 178 warheads with yields from sub-kiloton to several hundred kilotons, intended for Polish Army missile brigades and aviation regiments in wartime. The warheads themselves were always under exclusive Soviet 12th GUMO control; Polish forces were to receive them only on a Soviet release order. The facilities were handed over in 1969-70 and the last warheads were withdrawn around 1990-91 as the agreement lapsed. Two of the sites have been surveyed by archaeologists and are open to visitors; Podborsko houses a museum. Poland is today a NATO member that has repeatedly asked to join NATO's nuclear sharing arrangements - a request President Duda pressed publicly from 2022 and which remains unresolved.",
