  "status": "exhost",
  "narrative": "East Germany hosted the largest concentration of Soviet nuclear weapons outside the USSR itself - estimates of the peak run into the low thousands of warheads for the Group of Soviet Forces in Germany, its missile brigades, artillery, aircraft and air defences. The GDR paid for the two purpose-built warhead depots but never owned or controlled anything in them: Objekt 4000 at Stolzenhain near Juterbog, south of Berlin, and Objekt 4001 'Lychen-2' near Himmelpfort and Furstenberg to the north, both built by German workers around 1967 and handed to exclusively Soviet staff in 1968, each with hardened Granit- and Monolit-type storage bunkers behind multiple fences. Additional field storage existed at operational bases such as Larz. National People's Army units trained with nuclear-capable Soviet systems but the warheads stayed under 12th GUMO custody, and the Soviet Union withdrew them at the end of the 1980s without formally notifying the GDR government. The final Soviet withdrawal from unified Germany was completed by 1991-94. The two depots are now derelict woodland ruins, extensively documented by urban explorers and, in the Stolzenhain case, partly accessible as a memorial site. Unified Germany remains a NATO nuclear-sharing host at Buchel, in the west - the eastern territory is covered by the 1990 Two Plus Four Treaty's ban on stationing nuclear weapons there.",
