  "status": "latent",
  "latentFrom": 1990,
  "narrative": "Japan is the only country to have been attacked with nuclear weapons and the non-nuclear-weapon state with the largest latent capability. Wartime Japan ran two small, badly resourced weapons projects - the Army's Ni-Go under Yoshio Nishina at RIKEN in Tokyo and the Navy's F-Go at Kyoto Imperial University - which never got beyond thermal-diffusion separation experiments; American occupation forces destroyed RIKEN's cyclotrons in November 1945. Post-war Japan adopted the Three Non-Nuclear Principles in 1967, ratified the NPT in 1976 and built the most elaborate civil fuel cycle of any non-weapon state, uniquely obtaining US consent for large-scale reprocessing under the 1988 bilateral agreement. The result is a stockpile of about 45 tonnes of separated plutonium - roughly 8-9 tonnes held in Japan and the rest in the UK and France - held under full safeguards, and a nearly complete reprocessing plant at Rokkasho in Aomori with a design capacity of 800 tonnes of spent fuel and up to 8 tonnes of plutonium a year. Rokkasho has been delayed twenty-seven times since construction began in 1993; the January 2026 provisional plan foresees a first 0.6 tonnes of plutonium in the second half of FY2027 and 3.2 tonnes a year by FY2030. Japanese officials, including Shinzo Abe in 2002 and again in 2016-17, have argued that possessing nuclear weapons would not necessarily be unconstitutional,…",
