  "narrative": "Nazi Germany's Uranverein began within weeks of the discovery of fission and never came close to a weapon. Split between competing groups under Werner Heisenberg, Kurt Diebner and others, starved of resources after 1942 when Albert Speer concluded a bomb could not be ready in time, and crippled by an early error on graphite's suitability as a moderator - which forced reliance on Norwegian heavy water, itself sabotaged at Vemork - the programme ended with a subcritical experiment. The last attempt, B-VIII, was run in a cellar beneath the church at Haigerloch in the Swabian Alb in February-March 1945, using 664 uranium cubes on chains in a heavy-water tank; it reached about a sevenfold neutron multiplication, still short of criticality. The Allied Alsos mission seized the equipment in April 1945 and interned the leading physicists at Farm Hall in England, where their secretly recorded reaction to Hiroshima remains the definitive record of how far behind they were. The cellar is now the Atomkeller Museum. Post-war West Germany renounced the manufacture of nuclear weapons in the 1954 Paris Agreements creating the Western European Union - a renunciation Konrad Adenauer privately described as valid 'rebus sic stantibus' - and Franz Josef Strauss pursued a joint French-Italian-German weapons programme in 1957-58 that de Gaulle cancelled in 1958. West Germany ratified the NPT in…",
