  "status": "exhost",
  "narrative": "Australia hosted twelve British nuclear tests at the Montebello Islands, Emu Field and Maralinga between 1952 and 1957, plus hundreds of contaminating 'minor trials', and expected to share in the resulting weapons. When Britain turned back to the United States after the 1958 Mutual Defence Agreement, Canberra began looking at an independent option. The Australian Atomic Energy Commission at Lucas Heights, senior defence officials and Prime Minister John Gorton pushed hardest in 1968-71: the plan was a natural-uranium power reactor at Jervis Bay on the New South Wales coast, chosen partly because its fuel cycle could yield plutonium, with the site cleared and tenders called in 1969-70. Gorton was replaced in 1971 and the project was shelved in 1971 and cancelled in 1972; the excavated site is still visible. Australia signed the NPT in 1970 but delayed ratification until 23 January 1973 under the incoming Whitlam government, and has since become one of the strongest safeguards and export-control states, hosting the Australian Safeguards and Non-Proliferation Office and chairing the group that bears its name. The AUKUS agreement of 2021, under which Australia will operate nuclear-powered submarines fuelled with weapons-grade HEU supplied by the US and UK, has revived arguments about the safeguards precedent it sets, though Australia has no weapons programme and reaffirms its NPT…",
